We own this fork. Edits are normal code changes; we do not track upstream
automatically. To pull a specific upstream change later, cherry-pick from a
local clone of openai/codex into vendor/codex-rs/.

Montage workspace lints (unwrap_used=deny, etc.) are scoped to crates/* and
do not apply here.

Our edits (re-apply after every refresh):
- vendor/codex-rs/exec/src/cli.rs: `#[derive(Parser, Debug, Default)]` on `Cli`
  so montage-cli can construct one programmatically. See crates/cli/src/chat_codex_cmd.rs.
- vendor/codex-rs/login/src/auth/default_client.rs,
  vendor/codex-rs/login/src/auth/manager.rs,
  vendor/codex-rs/exec/src/lib.rs,
  vendor/codex-rs/exec/src/event_processor_with_human_output.rs,
  vendor/codex-rs/analytics/src/events.rs, and
  vendor/codex-rs/model-provider-info/src/lib.rs:
  use each vendored crate's package version for model-manager, telemetry,
  display, and provider-version metadata. The login User-Agent may use
  `MONTAGE_CODEX_CLI_VERSION` when a deployment has sanctioned Codex backend
  access and must preserve the compatible Codex client version expected by the
  backend. Earlier Montage revisions hardcoded an installed Codex CLI version
  to bypass model-access gates; public source now requires explicit deployment
  configuration instead of silently mimicking another client.
  ChatGPT OAuth refresh/revoke read `MONTAGE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`, the same
  sanctioned client id used by Montage's initial browser login flow, so public
  source does not embed another client's first-party id.
- vendor/codex-rs/models-manager/src/lib.rs: keep the Codex protocol/model
  discovery client version separate from Montage's workspace package version
  so `/models` filtering remains compatible with the vendored Codex engine.
- vendor/codex-rs/core/src/client.rs and vendor/codex-rs/core/src/turn_metadata.rs:
  keep Montage's current websocket metadata shape. Revisit this area when
  refreshing the fork against a sanctioned upstream client contract.
- vendor/codex-rs/exec/src/lib.rs: replace the hardcoded
  `approval_policy: Some(AskForApproval::Never)` in `ConfigOverrides`
  with a check that honors `config_toml.approval_policy` when the user
  has explicitly set one (via -c or config). Without this, `codex exec`
  unconditionally drops approvals in headless mode and per-tool MCP
  approval prompts never fire even when annotations and config say
  they should. Keeps `Never` as the default when the user hasn't set
  a policy, so existing non-interactive callers don't change behavior.
- vendor/codex-rs/tui/Cargo.toml + vendor/codex-rs/tui/src/lib.rs:
  add `montage-proto = { workspace = true }` and `mod montage;` for the
  step-6 side panel.
- vendor/codex-rs/tui/src/montage/{mod,timeline,project_insights}.rs:
  new module hosting the Montage panel. Always-visible policy:
  uses `MONTAGE_PROJECT_ROOT` when set, otherwise falls back to
  `current_dir()`. Renders to the right of the chat column (36 cols
  fixed). Pure ratatui widgets reading from disk; refresh by calling
  `MontagePanel::refresh()` from App event handling.
- vendor/codex-rs/tui/src/app.rs: `App` struct gains
  `montage_panel: Option<MontagePanel>` (initialized via
  `MontagePanel::detect()`); `render_chat_widget_frame` does a
  horizontal split when the panel is `Some`. Behavior is unchanged in
  vanilla codex sessions (env unset → panel is None → chat gets full
  width).
- vendor/codex-rs/Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock: deleted. The codex crates live
  inside Montage's single root workspace.

Upstream security drift process (added 2026-07-16, R13):
We do not track openai/codex automatically, but we do watch it for
security-relevant changes on a schedule:
- Cadence: weekly, via .github/workflows/codex-drift.yml (Mondays 09:00 UTC,
  plus workflow_dispatch for on-demand runs). The workflow is standalone —
  nothing depends on it, so a failure there never blocks CI, PRs, or
  releases. It fails visibly (job goes red, report in the job summary) only
  when scripts/codex-upstream-drift.sh flags security-relevant commits, so
  the signal is easy to spot in the Actions tab.
- What it does: scripts/codex-upstream-drift.sh shallow-clones
  github.com/openai/codex, lists commits touching codex-rs/ since the
  "Forked at commit" SHA recorded above, and flags any whose commit message
  matches security|CVE|RUSTSEC|vuln|sanitiz|escape|injection|overflow
  (case-insensitive) or whose changed paths look security-sensitive
  (core/src/safety, core/src/sandbox, sandbox/, exec/src/sandbox, landlock,
  seatbelt). It only reports — it never merges, cherry-picks, or modifies
  vendor/codex-rs.
- How to cherry-pick: when the report flags something worth pulling in,
  clone openai/codex locally (or reuse the workflow's report to identify the
  SHA), then `git -C <clone> show <sha> -- codex-rs/<path>` to inspect the
  change, and manually re-apply the equivalent edit inside
  vendor/codex-rs/<path> (a raw `git cherry-pick` won't apply cleanly since
  vendor/codex-rs/ is a subtree copy, not a git remote/subtree-merge). After
  cherry-picking, re-apply any of "our edits" listed above that touch the
  same files, and re-run the workspace's affected crate tests.
- Where SOURCE lives: this file (vendor/codex-rs/SOURCE) is the single
  source of truth for the fork SHA. Update "Forked at commit" and
  "Previous fork SHA" here whenever we do a full refresh of vendor/codex-rs
  from upstream — the drift script reads this file directly and has no
  other source of truth.
- Run it locally any time: `scripts/codex-upstream-drift.sh` (requires
  network access to github.com; set UPSTREAM_CLONE=/path/to/local/codex to
  run offline against a pre-fetched clone).
